  "body": "What happens: a STEP file whose DATA section holds one record with roughly 1000 or more nested `(` makes the import overflow the stack inside `ruststep`'s recursive-descent parser. The process dies - no error dialog, no diagnostic. It happens on main (a6bd69d8) with a valid header; found by the high-tier review of PR #1006 with a throwaway probe file (https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/pull/1006#pullrequestreview-5869273530). PR #1006 neither causes nor fixes it.\n\nWhy P0 (Prime Directive: \"Degenerate input (... corrupt STL/STEP models) -> clean error, not a crash or hang\"): opening a hostile or corrupt file takes the whole application down, unsaved work included. A stack overflow cannot be caught with `catch_unwind`.\n\nExpected: such a file is refused by name (\"STEP record nests deeper than N levels at entity #id, line L\"), like every other corrupt file.\n\nWays to fix, to weigh in the decision file: (a) a cheap pre-scan of the DATA section that tracks parenthesis depth (outside strings and comments) and refuses past a stated cap before the parser runs - PR #1006's `step_diag.rs` already walks records and could host it; (b) parse on a thread with a large, bounded stack and treat its death as the refusal; (c) both. The STEP importer through solidcore (#916 / PR #941) caps parser nesting at 64 already - check the same input there and say so.\n\nAlso check the same class in the other importers the fuzz tests cover (STL, DXF if present): any recursive parser fed by file depth.\n\nDone means: a fixture with a record nested 5000 deep is refused by name within a bounded time, in the default lane; a legitimately deep record under the cap still imports; the cap and its reason are recorded.\n",
